                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "H.1.3 Demands of the secondary actors",
            "block_bbox": [
                68,
                292,
                477,
                319
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    68.0,
                    292.0
                ],
                [
                    477.0,
                    292.0
                ],
                [
                    477.0,
                    319.0
                ],
                [
                    68.0,
                    319.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "A secondary actor (resp. the organization) that manages its PKI (public key infrastructure) typically has the general demands listed below. They result from the fact that the organizational overhead to manage a PKI should be kept small. This overhead increases if multiple companies or organizations coordinate their actions or when task cannot be distributed to multiple organizations because of the certificate structure defined in this document.",
            "block_bbox": [
                66,
                342,
                1052,
                473
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    66.0,
                    342.0
                ],
                [
                    1052.0,
                    342.0
                ],
                [
                    1052.0,
                    473.0
                ],
                [
                    66.0,
                    473.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "R4 In order to be able to distribute a common root certificate (that is for instance installed in each EV), the secondary actors build a group that uses a common (i.e. single) root certificate. All certificates created by these secondary actors are derived (indirectly) from this root certificate (i.e. the certificates are signed with the private key that belongs to this root certificate). It is difficult to achieve that all secondary actors (world-wide) work together in only one group. Probably, multiple groups are required for secondary actors of different continents or of different kind (e.g. operators of EVSEs, utilities, mobility providers). If many different groups are built it becomes easier to organize these groups (each with its own root certificate).",
            "block_bbox": [
                69,
                495,
                1054,
                706
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    69.0,
                    495.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    495.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    706.0
                ],
                [
                    69.0,
                    706.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "R5 A central organization of each group creates its root certificate and the corresponding (very secret) private key. It is not realistic that this organization creates (and signs) all certificates that are required by all secondary actors (e.g. all contract certificates of all customers). Instead, each secondary actor needs to have the possibility to create its certificates itself. For this purpose, it needs an \"intermediate certificate\" that is signed by the root certificate and used to sign the (leaf) certificates created by this secondary actor. That means, a chain of certificates is required. Furthermore, it is not realistic that the central (root) organization directly signs the \"intermediate certificates\" of all secondary actors. Instead, several \"intermediate organizations\" are needed (e.g. one for each country). This results in a certificate chain with 2 \"intermediate certificates\". If more \"intermediate certificates\" (i.e. levels for intermediate organizations) are used it becomes easier to manage the cooperation between the different organizations.",
            "block_bbox": [
                69,
                725,
                1054,
                1014
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    69.0,
                    725.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    725.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    1014.0
                ],
                [
                    69.0,
                    1014.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "R6 Communication between an SECC and an SA IT delays the start of the charging procedure and reduces the availability of the EVSE (since communication may fail). Therefore, such communication should be avoided whenever possible (from the perspective of an SA). Instead, it is preferred that an EVSE stays offline during a whole charging procedure.",
            "block_bbox": [
                69,
                1033,
                1054,
                1139
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    69.0,
                    1033.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    1033.0
                ],
                [
                    1054.0,
                    1139.0
                ],
                [
                    69.0,
                    1139.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE Demand R6 respects communication costs and delays before start of charging. However, there can be other priorities of the participants as well. Furthermore, there can exist scenarios where no relevant communication costs are created (e.g. if the EVSE possesses a LAN or WLAN connection to the internet (resp. its backend system)). For example in the use case [C2] of ISO 15118-1, the EVCC communicates directly with the secondary actor using a direct TLS channel. If it is intended that this communication link exists permanently during charging, demand R6 is not applicable. As another example, the SECC can offer an internet connection or a TCP/IP connection to an arbitrary server as (value added) service to the EV. This connection can exist the whole time during charging (or even longer) and results in communication traffic as well. In both examples, the implementation of the SECC will handle the communication traffic appropriately.",
            "block_bbox": [
                66,
                1160,
                1054,
                1375
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    66.0,
                    1160.0
                ],
